; ============================================================================
; WORKDESK -- the desk reports two workspaces and ONE NAME IN TWO AREAS
;
; Status: CANDIDATE / explicit-run.
; Safety: MUTATING (SANDBOX only; both fixtures are erased at both ends).
;         MINTS CATALOG ROWS -- WORKSPACE OPEN ... AS births one per
;         workspace, so the spec entry carries mints_catalog.
;
; WHY THIS SPEC EXISTS, and it is the first of its kind here: until 2026-09-10
; NOTHING asserted a byte of WSREPORT's output. It appears ZERO times in
; cmd_regression.cpp, the host carrying 81 specs, and it is `status: supported`.
; Its "Workspace" block could not have been asserted anyway -- it showed AREA
; slots under a workspace heading and never called the workspace table, so its
; content changed with whatever happened to be open and named no workspace at
; all. WORKDESK is assertable BECAUSE the desk is ordered on purpose: handles
; sorted ascending, DEFAULT always handle 1 and therefore always first.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE FIXTURE IS TWO DIRECTORIES, AND THE FIRST TWO CUTS OF THIS SPEC WERE
; WRONG ABOUT WHY.
;
; Both earlier cuts opened ONE file twice with `USE WDARM AGAIN` and expected
; WORKDESK's fanout section to appear. It never did, and the code was right.
; `USE ... AGAIN` is the ONE route that CANNOT produce a doubled name:
; cmd_use.cpp resolves the alias before it touches the area
; (find_open_area_by_alias -> derive_distinct_alias, cmd_use.cpp:472/492),
; renames the second instance to WDARM2 and SAYS SO on the console. Two
; handles, two names. The fanout pass keys on DbArea::name(), which IS the
; alias -- so by construction it can never fire on that path.
;
; Every OTHER opener skips that arm. WORKSPACE OPEN <dir> dedupes on PATH and
; not on name (cmd_workspace.cpp:1511); WORKSPACE ADD, WORKSPACE LOAD and both
; schema-restore paths apply a saved alias verbatim; CREATE and AUTODBF take
; the file stem. So the condition WORKDESK reports is the one that actually
; occurs: TWO DIFFERENT FILES SHARING A BASENAME, opened from two directories
; into two workspaces. That is how it was first seen -- x64\BUILDING.dbf and
; x32\BUILDING.dbf, in a live session, both answering to BUILDING.
;
; This spec reproduces that TOPOLOGY, not merely that condition. Bending the
; arm until the assertion went green -- forcing the collision with an explicit
; ALIAS clause -- was available and was refused: a spec adjusted to match what
; the code did stops meaning anything.
;
; AND IT MATTERS. The name-based resolvers are FIRST-WINS:
; cli::find_open_area_in_workspace_ci() returns the LOWEST matching slot
; (workarea_util.cpp:171) and SQL resolves table names through it. The second
; area is open and unreachable by name. WORKDESK does not refuse it and does
; not repair it; it says the name is doubled so a caller can qualify on
; purpose instead of taking first-wins by accident.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;
; FIXTURE DIRECTORIES. DBF/SANDBOX/R131A and .../R131B are the house's shared
; two-directory scratch pair. This spec BORROWS them rather than adding a
; sixth pair, and it can do so safely because fixtures are name-scoped: the
; pair's own spec (workspace_owns_its_environment.dts) builds and erases RSPT,
; this one builds and erases WDARM, and neither can see the other's files.
; Say the word and it gets a dedicated WDKA/WDKB pair instead -- that is a
; data-lane act, so it is not taken here.
;
; THEY MUST EXIST, and they are EMPTY DIRECTORIES AND GIT DOES NOT TRACK
; THOSE -- the same standing gap WAOA/WAOB, OJCA/OJCB, PKA/PKB and RPCA/RPCB
; already carry.
;
; RE-RUNNABLE WITHIN ONE SESSION, unlike the first two cuts. A workspace name
; cannot be reclaimed while it is live, so the earlier `WORKSPACE NEW WDESK2`
; made this a once-per-runtime spec and any second run went red at line one
; for a reason that was not about WORKDESK. The preamble now DESTROYS both
; names before it opens them, which is the idiom
; sqlsel_workspace_scope_regression.dts already uses.
;
; WHAT IS ASSERTED, and none of it is decoration:
;
;   WD_G0/WD_G1  the two fixtures are DIFFERENT FILES that happen to share a
;                basename -- read on their own, before any workspace exists.
;                Without these, "the desk reported a doubled name" cannot be
;                told from "the desk reported the same file twice".
;   WD_W0        area 0 belongs to WDKA and reads AAA.
;   WD_W1        THE DISCRIMINATOR. Area 1 belongs to WDKB and reads BBB. Two
;                areas carrying ONE NAME are two independent tables; if the
;                desk were reporting one file seen twice, both would read AAA.
;
; The rest is asserted against WORKDESK's own routed output by the validator:
; DEFAULT sorts first and is named in phase 1, the current-workspace marker
; follows SWITCH, the current AREA is reported as belonging to a workspace
; that is NOT the current one, the fanout section appears EXACTLY ONCE (phase
; 2 and not phase 1), and no I1 violation is reported.
;
; HANDLES AND WS_IDS ARE NEVER ASSERTED BY VALUE except DEFAULT's handle 1,
; which is an invariant. WDKA's and WDKB's handles depend on how many
; workspaces this session already made, and their WS_IDs are adopted from the
; catalog when the name has a live chain. Asserting either would make this
; spec fail for a reason that has nothing to do with WORKDESK.
; ============================================================================

SET ECHO OFF
STOP_ON_ERROR OFF

FORMULA "WORKDESK-ARM-BEGIN"

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; PREAMBLE. Destroy first, so a second run in the same runtime is a re-run and
; not a red line about a name that could not be reclaimed.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
WORKSPACE CLOSE ALL
WORKSPACE DESTROY WDKA
WORKSPACE DESTROY WDKB
WORKSPACE SWITCH DEFAULT

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; TWO FILES, ONE BASENAME, TWO DIRECTORIES. The marks differ on purpose: they
; are the only thing that can prove the desk is reporting two tables rather
; than one table counted twice.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX/R131A
ERASE TABLE WDARM CONFIRM
CREATE X64 WDARM (ID N(6,0), MARK C(8))
APPEND
REPLACE ID WITH 1
REPLACE MARK WITH "AAA"
CLOSE

SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX/R131B
ERASE TABLE WDARM CONFIRM
CREATE X64 WDARM (ID N(6,0), MARK C(8))
APPEND
REPLACE ID WITH 1
REPLACE MARK WITH "BBB"
CLOSE

WORKSPACE CLOSE ALL

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; GUARDS. Each fixture read alone, before a workspace exists. These are what
; make the two-area report meaningful later.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX/R131A
USE WDARM
? "WD_G0_fixture_a_reads_AAA:" + (ALLTRIM(MARK) = "AAA")
CLOSE

SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX/R131B
USE WDARM
? "WD_G1_fixture_b_reads_BBB:" + (ALLTRIM(MARK) = "BBB")
CLOSE

WORKSPACE CLOSE ALL
SET PATH DBF DBF
WORKSPACE SWITCH DEFAULT

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; PHASE 1 -- the desk with nothing of ours open.
; Establishes that the header names DEFAULT (handle 1 by invariant) and that
; the fanout section is ABSENT. Phase 2 is the SAME command after two
; workspaces hold two same-named tables.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
WORKDESK

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; TWO WORKSPACES, EACH ON ITS OWN DIRECTORY. `AS` is required as of R131 --
; deriving the workspace name from the directory leaf was withdrawn.
;
; This is the opener that does NOT uniquify the alias, which is the point:
; both areas come out named WDARM.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
WORKSPACE OPEN DBF/SANDBOX/R131A AS WDKA
WORKSPACE OPEN DBF/SANDBOX/R131B AS WDKB

SELECT 0
? "WD_W0_area0_is_workspace_a_and_reads_AAA:" + (ALLTRIM(MARK) = "AAA")

SELECT 1
? "WD_W1_area1_is_workspace_b_and_reads_BBB:" + (ALLTRIM(MARK) = "BBB")

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; STAND IN WDKB WITH THE CURSOR IN WDKA'S AREA. The divergence is legal --
; SWITCH moves the workspace, never the cursor -- and the desk is required to
; say so, so the NOTE line is asserted. The owning handle is deliberately not
; asserted by value; the sentence around it is.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
WORKSPACE SWITCH WDKB
SELECT 0

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; PHASE 2 -- the same command, now with two workspaces and a doubled name.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
WORKDESK

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; TEARDOWN. Symmetric with the preamble, so the next run starts clean whether
; or not this one reached the end.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
WORKSPACE CLOSE ALL
WORKSPACE DESTROY WDKA
WORKSPACE DESTROY WDKB
WORKSPACE SWITCH DEFAULT

SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX/R131A
ERASE TABLE WDARM CONFIRM
SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX/R131B
ERASE TABLE WDARM CONFIRM
SET PATH DBF DBF

FORMULA "WORKDESK-ARM-END"
